S gcc ANXIETY IN PHILADELPHIA
S cr [By Telegraph to The Tribune.]
@role b7
P Philadelphia, April 15.—Relatives of Phila-
  delphia passengers on board the Titanic
  stormed the local newspaper and cable of-
  fices and the offices of the White Star Line
  here this morning in an effort to get
  authentic news of the disaster.
P Most of the Philadelphia passengers of
  the Titanic are prominent financially and
  socially here and along the main line.
  They include:
P Mr. and Mrs. George D. Widener, of
  Lynnewood Hall, Elkins Park, and their
  son, Harry Elkins Widener.
P Mr. and Mrs. John B. Thayer and their
  son, John B. Thayer, Jr., of Haverford.
P William C. Dulles, of No. 319 South
  Twelfth street.
P Mr. and Mrs. William E. Carter and their
  son and daughter, William, jr., and Miss
  Lucille Carter, of Bryn Mawr.
P Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Ryerson, of Haver-
  ford, who are hurrying home to attend the
  funeral of their son, Arthur Larned Ryer-
  son, who was killed last Monday with J.
  Louis Hoffman, another Yale student, in an
  automobile accident.
P Mrs. J. W. M. Cardeza, of Washington
  Lane and Morton street, Germantown, and
  her son, T. D. M. Cardeza.
P Robert W. Daniel, of the banking firm
  of Shillard-Smith-Daniel Company, of No.
  328 Chestnut street.
P Mrs. Thomas Potter, widow of Colonel
  Thomas Potter, and her daughter, Mrs.
  Boulton Earnshaw, of Mt. Airy.
